.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Chess
shows a game of chess in a
.IR rio (1)
window: a board, and beside or under it the game's moves.
The
.I file
is one game in PGN, the Portable Game Notation:
tags such as
\fB[White "Morphy"]\fR
and the moves, with comments in braces,
annotations such as
.B $1
and
.BR !? ,
and variations in parentheses.
A
.B FEN
tag starts the game from that position.
A file that does not exist starts as a game with PGN's seven tags and no moves.
.PP
The board shows the position after the move at or before the end of dot,
so a selection of several moves, as Undo leaves, shows the last;
when another program replaces the whole text, as
.B "Edit ,|chess -a"
does, the board keeps the move it showed.
The list of moves marks the move at dot;
button 1 there goes to a move,
and a sweep selects a run of moves.
.I Chess
checks every move and writes each in standard algebraic notation (SAN),
with a file, a rank or both where two pieces could make it,
and
.B +
and
.B #
for check and mate.
It needs no engine for that.
A square is never smaller than twice the font's height.
In a window too narrow for that board, it is cut off at the right;
in one too short, it keeps its size and is cut off at its foot;
a scroll bar at the left, acme's, and the mouse wheel over the board move it,
and the line under it stays.
.SS Mouse and keys
Button 1 on a piece of the side to move marks the squares it can move to;
button 1 on one of those squares makes the move.
Dragging the piece there makes it too.
A pawn that reaches the last rank shows the pieces it can become, to choose with button 1.
A move made at the end of a line extends it, or starts the next line when it would make the line
longer than 79 characters.
A move made at an earlier move opens a variation:
.I chess
writes it in parentheses, numbered, after the move it replaces.
A move the game already has goes to that move instead.
.PP
Button 3 is a menu:
.B flip
turns the board,
.B hint
shows the engine's best move in this position alone,
.B analysis
and
.B play
turn those on and off,
.B eval
evaluates the moves selected,
.B write
writes the file, and
.B exit
ends
.IR chess ,
or says once that the file is not written.
Over the board the mouse wheel steps back and forward a move, when the whole board shows;
over the moves it scrolls them.
.PP
The left and right arrows step back and forward a move,
up and down go to the variation before and after,
Home goes to the start and End to the end of the line.
Typing a move makes it, in SAN
.RB ( e4 ,
.BR Nf3 ,
.BR exd5 ,
.BR O-O ,
.BR e8=Q ,
with or without
.BR x ,
.B +
and
.BR = ),
in squares
.RB ( e2e4 ,
.BR e7e8q ),
or as a piece and its square
.RB ( Nd2 ):
as it is typed, the line under the board shows it and the board marks the moves it can still be,
and once it is one move and complete,
.I chess
makes it.
A capital letter is a piece and a small one a file, unless no move fits that way;
.B o
and
.B 0
are castling's
.BR O .
Newline makes the one move a partial move can be, or the one it names in full, as
.B oo
when
.B O-O-O
is legal too; Backspace takes back a letter,
and Escape forgets what was typed and lets go of a piece;
.B ?
is
.BR hint .
.SS Engine
The
.I engine
is a command that
.IR rc (1)
runs in the background,
speaking UCI, the Universal Chess Interface,
on its standard input and output.
.I Chess
runs
.BR $chessengine ,
or without it
.BR "pkg run weiss -c weiss" :
Weiss, Terje Kirstihagen's engine, the
.B weiss
package (see
.IR pkg (1)).
With
.B -e
.B "''"
there is none.
.PP
Analysis starts off, so the game is yours to think through first, or on with
.BR -A .
While
.B analysis
is on, the engine searches the position at dot.
A bar left of the board shows how the game stands, white's share from white's side;
an arrow shows the engine's best move; and the line under the board gives
the evaluation, the depth, and the line of play it expects.
.I Chess
writes none of this to the game.
.B hint
in the menu, or the
.B ?
key, shows the same bar, arrow and line for the position at dot alone, until dot moves on.
In
.IR Chess ,
analysis also opens a window,
.IB file +Analysis\f1,
whose first line names the position at dot and the depth,
and whose lines after it are the engine's three best lines, each its evaluation and its moves;
button 2 on a move in one plays the line into the game from dot as far as that move, and on its evaluation the whole line,
and turning analysis off, or ending
.IR Chess ,
deletes the window.
A search stops after 30 seconds on one position.
.PP
.B Eval
runs
.B "chess -a"
(below) on the moves selected, or the move at dot,
from the position before them, and puts what it writes in their place,
as acme does with a command, whether it succeeds or fails:
an evaluation after each move, or, when it fails, the moves as they were.
What it says on standard error, and a failing status, go to
.IR chess 's
standard error.
.PP
When the text no longer parses as PGN,
the board stays as it last parsed, the line under it gives the error and its line,
and the board takes no move until the text parses again,
after an edit or
.BR Undo .
.PP
In
.BR play ,
the engine plays the side to move: it moves now, and answers each move after
by adding its own, in
.I ms
milliseconds.
.B Play
again stops it.
A game ends at checkmate, stalemate, a position neither side can mate from,
the fifth time a position comes again, or 75 moves without a capture or a pawn's move;
in play, also the third time or after 50.
Otherwise the third time and 50 moves are said under the board, as a draw either side can claim.
When the move that ends it is the last of the main line and the game's result is
.BR * ,
.I chess
writes the result after it and in the
.B Result
tag, in the same change as the move.
The analysis is on or off apart from play: while it is on, it shows on your moves, and the engine
gives it up to play its own.
.SS Without a window
With
.BR -s ,
.I chess
reads the game from
.I file
or standard input and writes it in PGN's export form:
spaces after move numbers, a black move numbered after a comment or variation,
SAN as it writes it, and lines of at most 79 characters.
An illegal move is an error, named with its line and column.
.PP
With
.BR -a ,
it reads a game, or moves alone, and writes it back with the engine's evaluation in a comment
after each move, in variations too, but a mate:
.B {+0.35}
in pawns from white's side, after
.I ms
milliseconds on the position, 1000 if not told, or
.B {#3}
and
.BR {#-3} ,
a mate in three for white and for black.
It replaces an evaluation already there.
It marks a move that costs its side 20 points of its chance of winning, out of 100, with
.BR ? ,
and 30 with
.BR ?? ,
the chances from the evaluations before and after the move, as lichess reckons them;
a move with a mark already keeps it.
The moves start from the game's
.B FEN
tag, or
.IR fen ,
or the start.
If they are not legal from there, or the engine fails or does not answer,
.I chess
writes its input back as it was, says why on standard error and fails,
so in
.IR acme (1)
.B "|chess -a"
on a selection, or
.BR "Edit ,|chess -a" ,
never loses a move.
.PP
With
.BR -d ,
it makes the moves on standard input, one a line, as button 1 does,
starting from
.IR file 's
start, and writes the game.
A move is in SAN or as its squares
.RB ( e2e4 ,
.BR e7e8q );
.B <
and
.B >
step back and forward,
.B [
and
.B ]
go to the variation before and after,
.B ^
to the start and
.B $
to the end of the line;
a line beginning with
.B '
types what follows it, as the keyboard does, and
.B '
alone types a newline.
.PP
With
.BR -p ,
it counts the positions
.I depth
moves on from
.I fen
or the start (perft), to test its moves.
.SS Chess
.I Chess
is
.I chess
in an
.IR acme (1)
window whose body is the game, as
.IR Sketch 's
is its picture.
With no
.IR file ,
it draws the window it is run from,
.BR $winid ;
with one, or outside a window, it opens a new window,
with the file's text if it exists.
Dot is acme's: stepping through the game moves it,
so the text shows the same move when
.I Chess
ends.
.I Chess
writes a move into the body as typing would,
so
.B Undo
takes it back and
.B Put
saves it.
When the body changes, by
.BR Undo ,
a pipe, or another program, the board follows it.
.PP
.I Chess
adds
.BR Chess ,
.BR Play ,
.B Analysis
and
.B "|chess -a"
to the tag, as words it does not have already.
.B Chess
there ends it, and the window shows its text, with
.B Chess
and
.B "|chess -a"
still in the tag and
.B Play
and
.B Analysis
gone;
.B Chess
there again draws the board.
.B Play
is
.BR play ;
.BR "|chess -a" ,
with arguments of its own if given, is
.BR eval ,
run from the position before the selection,
with
.B -e
and
.B -t
passed on;
and
.B Flip
and
.B Analysis
work too.
Other commands are acme's: a
.BR | ,
.B <
or
.B >
command works on the text at dot.
Command-Z and Command-Shift-Z are acme's
.B Undo
and
.BR Redo .
.I Chess
writes to the body only if no one else has changed it since it last read it;
if someone has, it says the file changed, reads it again, and leaves dot where it was.
.B Del
is ctl's
.BR del :
on a modified window acme says so in
.B +Errors
and leaves it; a second
.BR Del ,
or
.BR Delete ,
deletes the window, ending
.I Chess
and the engine with it.
A note that ends
.IR Chess ,
as
.B Kill
sends, ends the engine too.
.SH EXAMPLES
Evaluate a game's moves for half a second each:
.IP
.EX
chess -a -t 500 game.pgn >evaluated.pgn
.EE
.PP
Add Weiss to the name space and run it by name:
.IP
.EX
pkg add weiss
chessengine=weiss
Chess game.pgn
.EE
